# =============================================================================
# CODEOWNERS — who must review what.
#
# The load-bearing entries are the INFRASTRUCTURE ones. 06-FRONTEND-SEPARATION-
# PLAN.md requires that "proxy configuration [is] in an infrastructure-owned
# Compose/Caddy include that no application pipeline can write". That is
# enforced in three layers, and this file is one of them:
#
#   1. `docker/caddy` is mounted READ-ONLY into the caddy container.
#   2. `docker/deploy-app.sh` — the only deployment entry point an application
#      pipeline has — refuses the service names caddy, postgres, valkey, backup.
#   3. THIS FILE: a change to the proxy configuration, the production compose
#      file, the backup scripts or the CI pipelines requires an infrastructure
#      review. A marketing pull request that edits the Caddyfile cannot merge on
#      a marketing approval.
#
# Layers 1 and 2 are mechanical. This one is a review requirement and depends on
# branch protection having "require review from Code Owners" enabled — a setting
# that lives in the repository, not in the repository's files. Without it this
# file is documentation. Turn it on.
#
# Replace the placeholder handles with real ones before this means anything. A
# CODEOWNERS file naming a team that does not exist matches nothing and requires
# no review, which is the same class of silently-absent enforcement as an ESLint
# rule matching zero files.
# =============================================================================

# --- INFRASTRUCTURE ----------------------------------------------------------
/docker/                    @infrastructure
/docker/caddy/              @infrastructure
/docker/compose.prod.yml    @infrastructure
/docker/backup/             @infrastructure
/.github/                   @infrastructure
/tools/scan-secrets.mjs     @infrastructure

# The migration SQL and the schema: a schema change is a one-way door far more
# often than the diff suggests.
/backend/drizzle/           @infrastructure
/backend/src/platform/db/   @infrastructure

# --- APPLICATIONS ------------------------------------------------------------
/backend/src/modules/       @backend

# The frontend and the marketing site are not in this repository, so the rules
# that owned them are gone. A CODEOWNERS entry for a path that does not exist
# matches nothing and requires no review — the same silently-absent enforcement
# this file's header warns about.
